WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:08.000
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.879
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.879 --> 00:00:12.670
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.370
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.929
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:16.939 --> 00:00:18.979
things about the world at the same

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.720
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:23.799
I'm Alastair Budge, and today

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:26.722
we are going to be talking about

00:00:26.722 --> 00:00:28.969
the Victorian workhouse, the 19th

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.319
century institution which housed the

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.450
most desperate people in Britain.

00:00:33.939 --> 00:00:36.039
We'll learn why they were created,

00:00:36.289 --> 00:00:38.489
what life was really like inside

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.089
them, hear some shocking stories,

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.390
and learn how they were replaced

00:00:43.399 --> 00:00:45.539
by the welfare state we have today.

00:00:46.232 --> 00:00:48.442
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:48.452 --> 00:00:51.262
minute and get right into it.

00:00:52.672 --> 00:00:55.713
It is the winter of 1837.

00:00:56.332 --> 00:01:00.022
A small boy stands in a large stone hall.

00:01:00.792 --> 00:01:03.432
Around him, other children sit in

00:01:03.432 --> 00:01:05.852
rows, hunched over their bowls.

00:01:06.582 --> 00:01:09.742
The meal is gruel, which is water

00:01:10.032 --> 00:01:12.302
with a little oatmeal stirred into it.

00:01:12.792 --> 00:01:15.372
It's the same meal every day, which

00:01:15.372 --> 00:01:18.172
the boys devour out of necessity.

00:01:19.032 --> 00:01:21.782
The portions are measly, and their

00:01:21.782 --> 00:01:24.462
stomachs are again rumbling minutes

00:01:24.722 --> 00:01:26.312
after they have finished eating.

00:01:27.542 --> 00:01:30.707
One boy walks up to the master, nervously.

00:01:31.682 --> 00:01:32.812
He holds out his bowl.

00:01:33.922 --> 00:01:35.532
"Please, sir," he says.

00:01:36.372 --> 00:01:37.302
"I want some more."

00:01:37.952 --> 00:01:40.292
The master cannot believe the request.

00:01:40.772 --> 00:01:43.672
"What?", he asks, before declaring

00:01:43.862 --> 00:01:46.072
that the boy will be hanged one day.

00:01:47.312 --> 00:01:49.941
You may recognise this as the start

00:01:49.941 --> 00:01:52.562
of Charles Dickens' Oliver Twist.

00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:56.192
That image, this of a small,

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:59.102
frightened, starving child asking for

00:01:59.102 --> 00:02:01.562
a little more food, became one of the

00:02:01.562 --> 00:02:03.952
most famous in English literature.

00:02:04.822 --> 00:02:07.422
The location for this scene was

00:02:07.422 --> 00:02:10.192
of course the workhouse, Victorian

00:02:10.192 --> 00:02:12.582
Britain's answer to the question of

00:02:12.582 --> 00:02:15.662
what to do with the poorest in society.

00:02:16.642 --> 00:02:20.342
Now, to understand where workhouses come

00:02:20.342 --> 00:02:23.282
from, we need to go back a few hundred

00:02:23.282 --> 00:02:26.792
years to talk about how the British state

00:02:27.102 --> 00:02:29.721
dealt with the poorest people in society.

00:02:30.692 --> 00:02:33.042
The short answer is it didn't.

00:02:33.872 --> 00:02:35.892
For much of British history,

00:02:36.212 --> 00:02:38.192
if you were unable to work and

00:02:38.192 --> 00:02:40.492
support yourself, tough luck.

00:02:41.082 --> 00:02:43.642
You'd be reduced to begging, perhaps

00:02:43.682 --> 00:02:46.172
pushed into prostitution, or to

00:02:46.172 --> 00:02:48.572
relying on the charity of others.

00:02:49.872 --> 00:02:53.762
This was improved slightly in 1601,

00:02:53.962 --> 00:02:56.642
under Elizabeth I, when a law was

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:59.461
passed that made parishes — the

00:02:59.541 --> 00:03:02.041
groups of local churches — they

00:03:02.061 --> 00:03:04.652
were responsible for looking after

00:03:04.652 --> 00:03:07.282
the extreme poor in the local area.

00:03:07.692 --> 00:03:10.472
If you were destitute, you could apply

00:03:10.692 --> 00:03:13.272
for what was called outdoor relief,

00:03:13.432 --> 00:03:15.992
which was a small amount of money or food

00:03:16.292 --> 00:03:18.762
to help you survive in your own home.

00:03:19.722 --> 00:03:23.642
This was later expanded to even include

00:03:23.672 --> 00:03:26.802
the working poor, whereby parishes

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:29.282
topped up the wages of underpaid

00:03:29.282 --> 00:03:31.282
workers to ensure that they had enough

00:03:31.282 --> 00:03:33.012
money to support their families.

00:03:33.482 --> 00:03:35.532
It was imperfect.

00:03:35.552 --> 00:03:37.752
It was inconsistent, and it

00:03:37.752 --> 00:03:39.932
varied from parish to parish,

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:42.242
from one area to the next.

00:03:43.179 --> 00:03:46.079
But it allowed people to remain with

00:03:46.079 --> 00:03:48.329
their families in their own homes.

00:03:49.219 --> 00:03:51.259
The problem, as the politicians

00:03:51.289 --> 00:03:53.079
and economists of the time saw

00:03:53.079 --> 00:03:56.219
it, was it was too easy to get.

00:03:56.629 --> 00:03:58.449
It was, in their view,

00:03:58.789 --> 00:04:01.449
encouraging the poor to stay poor.

00:04:02.329 --> 00:04:04.019
If you could receive a little

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:05.999
money without working, why

00:04:05.999 --> 00:04:06.899
would you bother working?

00:04:08.079 --> 00:04:09.939
On the other side of the coin, it was

00:04:09.969 --> 00:04:12.739
also, they said, letting employers

00:04:12.749 --> 00:04:15.699
get away with paying low wages because

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:17.859
they knew that their workers would

00:04:17.859 --> 00:04:20.169
receive top-ups from the parish.

00:04:20.809 --> 00:04:23.079
And there was an ever increasing

00:04:23.079 --> 00:04:25.069
number of people in Britain who were

00:04:25.069 --> 00:04:27.619
forced to turn to this, either to

00:04:27.619 --> 00:04:30.049
request top-ups of their low wages,

00:04:30.409 --> 00:04:32.969
or who had fallen into destitution.

00:04:33.709 --> 00:04:36.519
By the early 19th century, Britain’s

00:04:36.519 --> 00:04:39.029
population had more than doubled,

00:04:39.319 --> 00:04:42.119
growing from six and a half million to

00:04:42.169 --> 00:04:45.039
fourteen million in just eighty years.

00:04:45.779 --> 00:04:47.229
The industrial revolution

00:04:47.439 --> 00:04:49.519
had transformed the economy.

00:04:49.959 --> 00:04:52.819
Factories had replaced cottage industries.

00:04:53.099 --> 00:04:55.349
Millions of people flooded

00:04:55.529 --> 00:04:57.159
from the countryside to the

00:04:57.159 --> 00:04:59.319
cities in search of work.

00:05:00.219 --> 00:05:02.259
In these cities, places like

00:05:02.479 --> 00:05:04.459
Manchester, Birmingham, and London,

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:07.549
work was irregular, housing was

00:05:07.589 --> 00:05:10.619
cramped and expensive, and millions

00:05:10.649 --> 00:05:13.489
of people were just a hair's breadth

00:05:13.689 --> 00:05:16.559
away from absolute destitution.

00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:20.389
The old structures of local charity and

00:05:20.429 --> 00:05:23.049
parish relief were never designed for

00:05:23.069 --> 00:05:26.379
this, and were creaking under the demand.

00:05:27.109 --> 00:05:31.959
Between 1780 and 1831, the cost of

00:05:32.039 --> 00:05:34.779
poor relief in England and Wales had

00:05:34.779 --> 00:05:38.029
roughly quadrupled, gone up four times.

00:05:38.729 --> 00:05:40.909
The money was going out, but

00:05:40.939 --> 00:05:42.679
the problem kept growing.

00:05:43.499 --> 00:05:47.629
So, in 1832, a Royal Commission

00:05:47.819 --> 00:05:50.169
was set up to investigate.

00:05:50.809 --> 00:05:53.519
Its key architect was a man named

00:05:53.639 --> 00:05:56.109
Edwin Chadwick, who was a follower

00:05:56.289 --> 00:05:58.459
of the philosopher Jeremy Bentham.

00:05:59.369 --> 00:06:02.229
As such, he believed in systems,

00:06:02.289 --> 00:06:05.209
in efficiency, in the idea that

00:06:05.209 --> 00:06:07.629
human behaviour could be shaped

00:06:07.889 --> 00:06:10.259
by designing the right incentives.

00:06:11.549 --> 00:06:13.719
The Commission's report came back with

00:06:13.719 --> 00:06:16.269
a series of suggestions about what to

00:06:16.269 --> 00:06:18.969
do about poverty, which were put into

00:06:18.999 --> 00:06:23.129
law with the Poor Law Amendment of 1834.

00:06:24.068 --> 00:06:26.209
And specifically, in terms of what we're

00:06:26.209 --> 00:06:30.059
talking about today, this led directly to

00:06:30.059 --> 00:06:33.729
the institutionalisation of the workhouse.

00:06:34.469 --> 00:06:38.349
Now, workhouses were not a new invention.

00:06:38.759 --> 00:06:41.169
They had existed in various forms

00:06:41.459 --> 00:06:44.699
since the early 1700s as places where

00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:46.989
the destitute could go to receive

00:06:46.999 --> 00:06:49.879
food and shelter in exchange for work.

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:52.679
But they were patchy.

00:06:53.078 --> 00:06:54.189
Inconsistently run.

00:06:54.479 --> 00:06:56.839
They varied enormously from

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:58.549
one parish to the next.

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:04.279
What the 1834 Act did was take this

00:07:04.379 --> 00:07:07.129
existing idea and transform it.

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:10.969
Every area of England and Wales

00:07:11.149 --> 00:07:13.039
would now have a workhouse.

00:07:13.379 --> 00:07:15.419
Every workhouse would operate

00:07:15.649 --> 00:07:17.749
on the same national principles.

00:07:18.889 --> 00:07:21.059
And those principles would be

00:07:21.069 --> 00:07:24.389
deliberately, intentionally harsh.

00:07:25.379 --> 00:07:28.329
At the heart of this was a principle with

00:07:28.329 --> 00:07:31.739
a rather cold name: "less eligibility."

00:07:32.689 --> 00:07:36.179
The conditions inside a workhouse must

00:07:36.179 --> 00:07:38.979
be worse than the conditions of the

00:07:38.979 --> 00:07:42.309
lowest independent labourer outside it.

00:07:43.309 --> 00:07:45.679
In other words, however bad it might

00:07:45.679 --> 00:07:48.809
be working the worst possible job that

00:07:48.809 --> 00:07:52.119
paid you the least possible salary, life

00:07:52.159 --> 00:07:55.249
in the workhouse needed to be worse.

00:07:56.269 --> 00:07:58.159
The logic was that it needed to

00:07:58.159 --> 00:08:00.629
be a genuine deterrent, which only

00:08:00.629 --> 00:08:03.219
the truly destitute would turn to.

00:08:03.879 --> 00:08:06.019
The state would help in this case,

00:08:06.089 --> 00:08:08.639
but it would make absolutely certain

00:08:08.869 --> 00:08:10.184
you did not want to be there.

00:08:11.339 --> 00:08:13.719
It was designed like this because it had

00:08:13.719 --> 00:08:17.509
a specific target in mind: the able-bodied

00:08:17.529 --> 00:08:21.259
man who was choosing idleness over work.

00:08:22.149 --> 00:08:24.948
Force him into a place unpleasant

00:08:24.949 --> 00:08:26.869
enough, so the argument went,

00:08:27.379 --> 00:08:29.129
and he would find a job.

00:08:30.129 --> 00:08:32.769
But the majority of people who

00:08:32.799 --> 00:08:34.949
actually ended up inside the

00:08:34.949 --> 00:08:37.589
workhouse were not idle young men.

00:08:38.549 --> 00:08:41.619
The majority were the elderly, who had

00:08:41.829 --> 00:08:43.829
outlived their ability to earn a living.

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:46.759
They were the chronically sick, who could

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:49.439
not work because of illness or disability.

00:08:49.759 --> 00:08:52.525
They were children, many of them orphans.

00:08:52.965 --> 00:08:55.569
And they were women: pregnant women,

00:08:56.019 --> 00:08:58.989
unmarried mothers, widows, or women

00:08:59.069 --> 00:09:00.789
whose husbands had abandoned them.

00:09:01.929 --> 00:09:04.390
There were about 600 workhouses

00:09:04.639 --> 00:09:06.669
across England and Wales, with

00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:09.819
an estimated 150,000 people

00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:12.279
living in them at any one point.

00:09:13.259 --> 00:09:15.379
And life inside, well,

00:09:15.519 --> 00:09:17.319
it was incredibly grim.

00:09:17.989 --> 00:09:20.129
Men and women were separate.

00:09:20.429 --> 00:09:22.459
Children were separated from

00:09:22.479 --> 00:09:25.019
parents, brothers from sisters.

00:09:25.539 --> 00:09:28.179
Their clothes would be taken away, to

00:09:28.179 --> 00:09:31.069
be replaced by a rough grey uniform.

00:09:31.979 --> 00:09:33.959
The diet inside was set

00:09:34.169 --> 00:09:35.579
by a national schedule.

00:09:36.269 --> 00:09:39.129
Breakfast was gruel, every morning.

00:09:39.569 --> 00:09:41.359
Lunch was bread and

00:09:41.499 --> 00:09:42.929
perhaps a little cheese.

00:09:43.519 --> 00:09:45.809
In the evening, a thin broth,

00:09:45.959 --> 00:09:48.449
or very occasionally a small

00:09:48.449 --> 00:09:49.849
portion of cooked meat.

00:09:50.529 --> 00:09:52.439
Suet pudding might appear

00:09:52.479 --> 00:09:53.969
once or twice a week.

00:09:54.759 --> 00:09:57.639
And as for the quantities, they were

00:09:57.649 --> 00:10:00.459
calculated to be just enough to stop

00:10:00.489 --> 00:10:03.019
you dying of hunger, but nothing more.

00:10:03.989 --> 00:10:05.809
And no, you certainly couldn't

00:10:05.849 --> 00:10:07.409
ask for a second helping.

00:10:08.319 --> 00:10:10.829
And when you weren't eating your meagre

00:10:10.849 --> 00:10:13.449
rations of food, you were working,

00:10:13.819 --> 00:10:17.159
long hours doing hard, tedious work.

00:10:18.329 --> 00:10:20.469
One of the most common tasks

00:10:20.659 --> 00:10:22.849
given to women and children was

00:10:22.979 --> 00:10:25.549
unpicking old rope, separating the

00:10:25.559 --> 00:10:27.689
fibres so it could be used again.

00:10:28.399 --> 00:10:30.419
You had to use your bare fingers,

00:10:30.569 --> 00:10:33.639
and it was slow, relentless work.

00:10:34.029 --> 00:10:36.699
Your fingertips would crack and bleed.

00:10:37.339 --> 00:10:38.389
And it never ended.

00:10:38.659 --> 00:10:40.389
Once you finished one piece of rope,

00:10:40.663 --> 00:10:42.593
there was always another to be done.

00:10:43.683 --> 00:10:46.673
Men were often set to breaking stones

00:10:46.833 --> 00:10:49.623
to lay on roads, or to grinding bones

00:10:49.843 --> 00:10:51.713
that would be used as fertiliser.

00:10:52.383 --> 00:10:55.013
Or perhaps to working the treadmill,

00:10:55.233 --> 00:10:57.733
which was a large paddle wheel that you

00:10:57.773 --> 00:11:00.483
climbed continuously for hours at a time.

00:11:01.283 --> 00:11:03.373
The treadmill had originally been

00:11:03.373 --> 00:11:06.553
used to pump water or grind grain.

00:11:07.003 --> 00:11:10.593
But by this period, in many workhouses,

00:11:10.763 --> 00:11:13.389
it served no productive purpose at all.

00:11:13.853 --> 00:11:15.823
It was used simply because it

00:11:15.823 --> 00:11:18.163
was exhausting and unpleasant.

00:11:18.933 --> 00:11:21.503
In terms of the architecture of these

00:11:21.503 --> 00:11:24.213
workhouses, many were built around

00:11:24.223 --> 00:11:26.603
the same principles of order and

00:11:26.603 --> 00:11:28.803
control that you have in prisons,

00:11:28.973 --> 00:11:31.193
where guards can stand in the centre

00:11:31.463 --> 00:11:33.743
and observe what everyone is doing.

00:11:34.273 --> 00:11:35.413
Open dormitories.

00:11:35.703 --> 00:11:37.423
Shared dining halls with

00:11:37.433 --> 00:11:39.523
long, visible sight lines.

00:11:39.893 --> 00:11:42.113
A layout built around the idea

00:11:42.373 --> 00:11:44.933
that the master and matron could

00:11:44.933 --> 00:11:47.273
see everything, and that inmates

00:11:47.473 --> 00:11:49.396
knew there was nowhere to hide.

00:11:50.236 --> 00:11:52.456
Importantly, it wasn't a prison.

00:11:52.666 --> 00:11:54.526
You could leave, in theory.

00:11:54.916 --> 00:11:57.416
But you had to give notice, and

00:11:57.426 --> 00:11:59.376
in many cases wait twenty-four

00:11:59.406 --> 00:12:01.286
hours before being discharged.

00:12:02.096 --> 00:12:05.029
And more importantly, you left with

00:12:05.029 --> 00:12:08.199
nothing: no money, no food, nothing

00:12:08.199 --> 00:12:10.499
to support you in the outside world.

00:12:11.379 --> 00:12:13.699
For the elderly, the chronically

00:12:13.749 --> 00:12:16.039
ill, and the very young, there

00:12:16.039 --> 00:12:18.029
was often nowhere else to go.

00:12:18.749 --> 00:12:21.659
They entered, they stayed, and

00:12:21.869 --> 00:12:23.839
in many cases, they died there.

00:12:25.149 --> 00:12:28.039
And the conditions inside were no secret;

00:12:28.439 --> 00:12:31.399
everyone knew what lay behind those

00:12:31.419 --> 00:12:34.179
gates, and most people did everything

00:12:34.179 --> 00:12:36.433
they possibly could to avoid it.

00:12:37.799 --> 00:12:40.869
Yet in some workhouses, the reality

00:12:41.009 --> 00:12:43.579
was worse than anyone imagined.

00:12:44.589 --> 00:12:48.499
In 1845, a small item appeared

00:12:48.519 --> 00:12:50.379
in a local Hampshire newspaper.

00:12:50.929 --> 00:12:54.059
It concerned the workhouse in the market

00:12:54.059 --> 00:12:56.779
town of Andover, in southern England.

00:12:57.969 --> 00:13:00.759
Its inmates were mostly agricultural

00:13:00.759 --> 00:13:03.009
labourers, men and women who had

00:13:03.019 --> 00:13:05.349
worked the land their whole lives

00:13:05.419 --> 00:13:08.529
and had fallen into destitution when

00:13:08.549 --> 00:13:11.119
work dried up, or when illness or

00:13:11.149 --> 00:13:13.369
injury made them unable to continue.

00:13:14.619 --> 00:13:17.899
They had been set to work grinding bones.

00:13:18.359 --> 00:13:21.609
Old animal bones, mostly horse bones,

00:13:21.899 --> 00:13:24.466
collected from farms and slaughterhouses.

00:13:24.979 --> 00:13:26.879
You reduced them to powder

00:13:27.119 --> 00:13:29.489
using a heavy metal rod, and the

00:13:29.489 --> 00:13:31.929
powder was sold as fertiliser.

00:13:33.439 --> 00:13:35.789
The newspaper report alleged

00:13:36.019 --> 00:13:38.629
that the inmates were so hungry

00:13:39.069 --> 00:13:40.829
that they were fighting over the

00:13:40.829 --> 00:13:42.799
bones before they ground them.

00:13:43.179 --> 00:13:45.099
They were biting and gnawing at them.

00:13:45.419 --> 00:13:46.609
Sucking out the marrow.

00:13:46.969 --> 00:13:49.299
Scraping off whatever scraps of

00:13:49.299 --> 00:13:51.419
rotting meat and gristle remained.

00:13:52.999 --> 00:13:54.599
The story reached London,

00:13:54.819 --> 00:13:56.829
and it was a scandal.

00:13:57.549 --> 00:13:58.829
These were not starving

00:13:58.829 --> 00:14:01.179
people outside the system.

00:14:01.539 --> 00:14:02.589
These were starving people

00:14:02.749 --> 00:14:05.429
inside it, being theoretically

00:14:05.649 --> 00:14:07.569
cared for by the state.

00:14:08.729 --> 00:14:11.229
A parliamentary committee was set up.

00:14:11.439 --> 00:14:13.919
And what it found was worse than

00:14:13.919 --> 00:14:15.329
the newspaper had suggested.

00:14:16.699 --> 00:14:18.239
The master of the Andover

00:14:18.239 --> 00:14:20.649
workhouse was a man named Colin

00:14:20.649 --> 00:14:22.979
McDougal, a former army sergeant.

00:14:23.779 --> 00:14:25.349
He had been, the committee

00:14:25.349 --> 00:14:27.299
found, habitually drunk.

00:14:27.609 --> 00:14:29.509
He had been brutal towards inmates,

00:14:29.729 --> 00:14:32.299
using physical violence against men and

00:14:32.299 --> 00:14:34.759
women, and sexually abusing the women.

00:14:35.859 --> 00:14:38.729
One witness testified that the inmates

00:14:38.739 --> 00:14:41.509
had been so hungry that when a horse

00:14:41.569 --> 00:14:43.559
died in the yard, they had been

00:14:43.559 --> 00:14:45.789
grateful, because it meant more bones

00:14:45.789 --> 00:14:48.619
to grind, and more scraps to fight over.

00:14:49.549 --> 00:14:51.379
A woman testified that she had been

00:14:51.379 --> 00:14:53.409
too frightened to report anything,

00:14:53.779 --> 00:14:56.289
because to report the master was to

00:14:56.289 --> 00:14:59.239
risk punishment, and in the workhouse,

00:14:59.479 --> 00:15:01.899
the master controlled everything: your

00:15:01.899 --> 00:15:04.428
food, your sleeping arrangements, and

00:15:04.449 --> 00:15:06.379
your ability to see your children.

00:15:06.999 --> 00:15:09.299
The workhouse master was dismissed.

00:15:09.396 --> 00:15:11.586
There were some minor changes to the law.

00:15:12.256 --> 00:15:15.056
But the men and women of the Andover

00:15:15.056 --> 00:15:17.976
workhouse went back to grinding bones.

00:15:19.466 --> 00:15:22.386
Now, almost from the creation of

00:15:22.386 --> 00:15:25.276
the workhouse laws, critics had

00:15:25.276 --> 00:15:28.116
been arguing that it was inhumane,

00:15:28.596 --> 00:15:31.576
unnecessarily predatory towards those

00:15:31.626 --> 00:15:34.156
who were already at rock bottom.

00:15:34.946 --> 00:15:37.766
The argument for it was always

00:15:37.766 --> 00:15:39.916
that it was a deterrent, that in

00:15:39.916 --> 00:15:42.756
making the workhouse so awful you

00:15:42.756 --> 00:15:44.696
would ensure a strong incentive

00:15:44.936 --> 00:15:46.676
for people to support themselves.

00:15:47.616 --> 00:15:49.416
Or to put it another way, if

00:15:49.416 --> 00:15:51.366
you made poverty easier and more

00:15:51.366 --> 00:15:53.456
comfortable, you would encourage it.

00:15:55.126 --> 00:15:57.686
The reality is that it was an absolute

00:15:57.756 --> 00:16:00.346
last resort for practically everyone.

00:16:01.516 --> 00:16:04.026
You might remember a lady who was forced

00:16:04.036 --> 00:16:06.006
to send her sons to the workhouse,

00:16:06.076 --> 00:16:08.486
from an episode early this year.

00:16:09.806 --> 00:16:14.096
In 1896, a music hall singer had fallen

00:16:14.106 --> 00:16:17.306
on particularly hard times, and with

00:16:17.316 --> 00:16:20.416
nobody to turn to and no way to support

00:16:20.416 --> 00:16:22.876
her sons, she made the agonising

00:16:22.896 --> 00:16:26.036
decision to send them to a workhouse.

00:16:27.013 --> 00:16:28.776
Her older son was twelve.

00:16:29.166 --> 00:16:30.566
Her younger son was seven.

00:16:31.606 --> 00:16:34.096
The woman's name was Hannah, and her

00:16:34.096 --> 00:16:37.076
younger son was Charlie, Charlie Chaplin.

00:16:37.666 --> 00:16:39.976
Charlie Chaplin would later describe

00:16:39.996 --> 00:16:42.176
that morning in his autobiography.

00:16:43.006 --> 00:16:44.926
His mother had tried to keep their

00:16:44.926 --> 00:16:47.286
spirits up on the walk to the workhouse.

00:16:47.589 --> 00:16:49.806
But the boys, he wrote,

00:16:50.076 --> 00:16:51.406
could sense her despair.

00:16:52.526 --> 00:16:54.146
And then, at the workhouse

00:16:54.166 --> 00:16:55.636
gates, they were parted.

00:16:56.786 --> 00:16:59.816
Charlie and his brother to the boys' ward.

00:17:00.556 --> 00:17:02.096
Their mother to the women's.

00:17:02.876 --> 00:17:04.706
Chaplin said later that the

00:17:04.706 --> 00:17:06.536
humiliations of those years

00:17:06.776 --> 00:17:08.626
were what made him who he was.

00:17:09.036 --> 00:17:11.636
He went on to become, by many measures,

00:17:11.786 --> 00:17:14.326
the most famous man in the world, and

00:17:14.456 --> 00:17:15.596
if you'd like to hear more about his

00:17:15.596 --> 00:17:19.146
life, episode 598 is the one for you.

00:17:19.816 --> 00:17:21.715
Now, fortunately for Charlie

00:17:22.036 --> 00:17:23.576
he didn't stay in the workhouse

00:17:23.586 --> 00:17:26.516
forever, and the entire system itself

00:17:26.706 --> 00:17:28.706
would soon start to be dismantled.

00:17:29.476 --> 00:17:32.006
Reformers had been pushing for a total

00:17:32.006 --> 00:17:34.906
abolition of the workhouse system, to

00:17:34.906 --> 00:17:37.996
be replaced with proper state services

00:17:38.006 --> 00:17:40.606
for health, unemployment, and old age.

00:17:41.226 --> 00:17:43.156
And they came eventually.

00:17:43.806 --> 00:17:47.426
Old age pensions arrived in 1908, meaning

00:17:47.426 --> 00:17:50.116
the elderly no longer had to choose

00:17:50.116 --> 00:17:52.476
between the workhouse and starvation.

00:17:53.376 --> 00:17:56.266
Then in 1911 came what was

00:17:56.266 --> 00:17:58.016
called national insurance.

00:17:58.756 --> 00:18:02.046
The idea was that workers, employers, and

00:18:02.056 --> 00:18:04.276
the government would each contribute a

00:18:04.296 --> 00:18:07.176
small amount every week to a shared fund.

00:18:07.816 --> 00:18:09.996
And if you then lost your job

00:18:09.996 --> 00:18:12.056
or fell seriously ill, you

00:18:12.056 --> 00:18:14.126
could draw from that fund.

00:18:14.866 --> 00:18:16.826
This still exists today, by the

00:18:16.826 --> 00:18:19.186
way; if you work in the UK you'll

00:18:19.186 --> 00:18:21.086
know that every month a portion

00:18:21.096 --> 00:18:23.706
of your salary is set aside for

00:18:23.736 --> 00:18:25.606
"National Insurance contributions".

00:18:25.936 --> 00:18:27.736
It's pretty much the same thing.

00:18:28.596 --> 00:18:30.826
Now, even after the introduction

00:18:30.826 --> 00:18:32.726
of pensions and of national

00:18:32.726 --> 00:18:35.036
insurance, the workhouse remained.

00:18:35.836 --> 00:18:37.536
The reason was that neither

00:18:37.536 --> 00:18:39.216
reform was universal.

00:18:39.726 --> 00:18:42.646
National insurance in 1911 only

00:18:42.646 --> 00:18:44.255
covered workers in regulated

00:18:44.306 --> 00:18:46.486
employment in specific industries.

00:18:46.756 --> 00:18:49.039
It did not cover casual labourers,

00:18:49.186 --> 00:18:51.546
domestic servants, or the self-employed.

00:18:52.466 --> 00:18:54.216
Old age pensions were only

00:18:54.216 --> 00:18:56.066
paid to those over seventy.

00:18:56.376 --> 00:18:59.266
Anyone who fell outside those categories

00:18:59.586 --> 00:19:02.126
still had no alternative to the workhouse.

00:19:02.946 --> 00:19:06.386
It was not until 1948, with the National

00:19:06.426 --> 00:19:09.546
Assistance Act and the formal creation of

00:19:09.546 --> 00:19:12.476
the welfare state, that the Poor Law, and

00:19:12.486 --> 00:19:14.946
the workhouse, were finally abolished.

00:19:15.596 --> 00:19:18.406
Today, a pregnant single mother, an

00:19:18.446 --> 00:19:21.406
orphaned child or a man too old to

00:19:21.406 --> 00:19:24.356
work is no longer forced to ring the

00:19:24.356 --> 00:19:26.856
bell outside the workhouse gates; there

00:19:26.856 --> 00:19:29.826
are systems and safeguards in place to

00:19:29.826 --> 00:19:32.836
ensure that everyone, in theory, has

00:19:32.846 --> 00:19:35.236
the right to a dignified existence.

00:19:36.016 --> 00:19:38.366
Yet the discussions about how to

00:19:38.376 --> 00:19:41.276
do that are much the same as those

00:19:41.276 --> 00:19:42.966
that were going on 200 years ago.

00:19:43.486 --> 00:19:46.436
How does a society support people who

00:19:46.486 --> 00:19:48.486
cannot support themselves, without

00:19:48.486 --> 00:19:51.006
creating a system that some politicians

00:19:51.006 --> 00:19:53.566
and taxpayers see as unfair, or

00:19:53.566 --> 00:19:55.866
that creates a disincentive to work?

00:19:56.406 --> 00:19:58.726
And how do you make that judgement

00:19:58.946 --> 00:20:01.366
when poverty is caused not only

00:20:01.366 --> 00:20:04.176
by unemployment, but by illness,

00:20:04.446 --> 00:20:07.086
old age, low wages, insecure

00:20:07.165 --> 00:20:09.185
work and high housing costs?

00:20:10.026 --> 00:20:12.246
Like the Royal Commission back in

00:20:12.246 --> 00:20:15.426
1832, there are those who argue

00:20:15.776 --> 00:20:18.556
that it is now too easy to fall back

00:20:18.596 --> 00:20:21.096
on state support, and that state

00:20:21.096 --> 00:20:23.295
support is so great that there is

00:20:23.295 --> 00:20:25.606
often not much financial difference

00:20:25.666 --> 00:20:27.816
between working and not working.

00:20:28.676 --> 00:20:30.726
And of course there are plenty of people

00:20:30.876 --> 00:20:33.316
who say this is nonsense, and that

00:20:33.326 --> 00:20:35.726
these people want to work, but there

00:20:35.726 --> 00:20:37.526
simply aren't sufficient jobs for them.

00:20:38.716 --> 00:20:40.446
Fortunately for everyone

00:20:40.446 --> 00:20:42.796
concerned, nobody is suggesting

00:20:42.936 --> 00:20:44.446
the revival of the workhouse.

00:20:44.809 --> 00:20:47.056
That has been forever consigned

00:20:47.226 --> 00:20:49.136
to the pages of Oliver Twist.

00:20:50.826 --> 00:20:52.546
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:20:52.566 --> 00:20:55.159
episode on Victorian workhouses.

00:20:55.246 --> 00:20:57.726
We've made quite a few related episodes:

00:20:58.076 --> 00:21:01.395
episode number 598 on Charlie Chaplin,

00:21:01.415 --> 00:21:02.926
there's even an episode on Jeremy

00:21:02.936 --> 00:21:06.296
Bentham, which is episode number 116.

00:21:07.434 --> 00:21:09.164
You've been listening to English Learning

00:21:09.164 --> 00:21:11.814
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:21:12.144 --> 00:21:15.094
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:21:15.104 --> 00:21:17.034
I'll catch you in the next episode.

